// Practice quiz: lambdas as handlers
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters and treats colon, equals, hash, tilde and braces as syntax, so those are escaped; the lambda arrow is written in words.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/http-and-javalin/lambdas-as-handlers

::o1 what a lambda keeps::You rewrite an anonymous Handler class as a lambda. Which parts of the anonymous class do you keep? {
~The @Override line and the method body#Not quite. @Override is part of the ceremony a lambda drops.
~The words new Handler() and the body#Not quite. The interface comes from where the lambda is used, so new Handler() is not written.
~Everything, written on one line#Not quite. A lambda drops everything except the parameters and the body.
=The parameter name and the body of its one method#Correct. The compiler knows the rest from the interface\: new Handler(), @Override, public void handle and the parameter's type.
}

::o1 lambda with no parameters::An anonymous Runnable has one method, run(), with no parameters, and its body calls refresh(). How does the lambda start? {
~With r, then the arrow, then refresh()#Not quite. run() takes no parameters, so there is no name to put before the arrow.
=With empty parentheses, then the arrow, then refresh()#Correct. A lambda for a method with no parameters writes () before the arrow.
~With run(), then the arrow, then refresh()#Not quite. A lambda never names the method it implements.
~With \{ refresh(); \} and no arrow#Not quite. A lambda always has a parameter list and an arrow before its body.
}

::o2 where a lambda can stand::Why can Javalin's routes.get accept a lambda as its handler? {
~Javalin turns any lambda into a handler at runtime#Not quite. The compiler decides it, and only because Handler has one abstract method.
~Because Handler is a class with a default constructor#Not quite. Handler is an interface. Lambdas implement interfaces with one abstract method.
=Its handler parameter is Handler, a functional interface with exactly one abstract method#Correct. A lambda can only stand where Java expects a functional interface, and the lambda becomes that one method.
~Because every lambda in Java is a Handler#Not quite. The same lambda shape could implement another functional interface; the expected type decides.
}

::o2 counting abstract methods::An interface declares one abstract method apply, one default method describe, and the abstract method boolean equals(Object o). Is it a functional interface? {
~No; it has two abstract methods#Not quite. An abstract method matching a public method of Object, such as equals, does not count.
~No; a functional interface cannot have a default method#Not quite. Default methods are allowed; they simply do not count.
~Only if it has the @FunctionalInterface annotation#Not quite. The annotation is optional. It only makes the compiler check the rule.
=Yes; only apply counts, because default methods and public Object methods such as equals do not#Correct. Default methods already have bodies, and every object already has equals.
}

::o3 when a method reference fits::Which lambda can be replaced by the method reference Audit\:\:record? {
=s, then the arrow, then Audit.record(s)#Correct. The lambda only calls one existing method with its own parameter, so Audit\:\:record does the same.
~s, then the arrow, then Audit.record(s.trim())#Not quite. The lambda changes the argument before the call, so a plain method reference would pass s unchanged.
~s, then the arrow, then a block that calls Audit.record(s) and then save(s)#Not quite. The lambda does two things; a method reference names exactly one method.
~s, then the arrow, then Audit.record("done")#Not quite. The lambda ignores its own parameter, so it is not a plain call with the lambda's parameters.
}

::o3 reference, not a call::A route is registered with ReportController\:\:summary. When does summary run? {
~Once, when the route is registered#Not quite. Writing the reference does not call the method. No parentheses or arguments are written.
=Later, once for each request to that route#Correct. A method reference names the method without calling it; Javalin calls it per request.
~Never, because no arguments were passed#Not quite. Javalin passes the Context itself each time it calls the method.
~Only when the server starts#Not quite. Javalin calls the handler for each matching request, not at start-up.
}
